        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(4,9):$\n\n- Multiples of $4:$ $\\,4,\\,8,\\,12,\\,16,\\,20,\\,24,\\,28,\\,32,\\,36,\u2026$\n\n- Multiples of $9:$ $\\,9,\\,18,\\,27,\\,36,\\,45,\\,54,\u2026$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(4,9)=36.$\n\nSince the variable part is the same in the expressions, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((y-3),\\,(y-3))=(y-3).\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(4(y-3),\\,9(y-3))=36\\cdot (y-3)=36(y-3).\n$$"
      },
